-\textbf{Contributions.} This paper makes four specific contributions: (1) \emph{Controlled memory-contamination protocol}: a systematic framework for injecting authority-markers at controlled contamination probabilities, enabling causal comparison of Append-Only, Summarization, and relevance-filtered RAG architectures under matched conditions. (2) \emph{Distributional evaluation metric}: the Wasserstein-1 distance $\Gamma_{\text{temporal}}$ between biased and clean output-length distributions, with explicit definition, calculation method, and bootstrap confidence intervals. (3) \emph{Model-version-dependent architecture ranking}: a complete two-model factorial (DeepSeek-V4-Pro and V4-Flash, 30 seeds per cell) demonstrating that Summarization ranks highest on both Round-4 models, opposite to the R1 V4-Chat ranking, with explicit reporting of ranking reversals. (4) \emph{Full reproducibility package}: all experiment code, raw API logs, contamination injection scripts, and analysis code released with deterministic seeds (PYTHONHASHSEED=0), SHA-256 checksums, and step-by-step reproduction protocols in the supplementary material.\section{Experimental Setup}
-\label{sec:experimental-setup}
-
-\textbf{Models and APIs.} All experiments use the following configuration unless otherwise noted. \textit{Primary model}: DeepSeek-V4-Chat (API version deepseek-chat, 2024-12 release) with temperature $=0.7$, top\_p $=0.9$, and max\_tokens $=2048$ for all agent interactions. \textit{Cross-model validation}: DeepSeek-V4-Pro and DeepSeek-V4-Flash via their respective APIs with identical generation parameters. \textit{Reproducibility}: all randomness controlled via deterministic seeds (seed $=42$ for canonical runs; 10 distinct seeds per cell for the length-injection grid, 30 seeds per cell for the model-by-architecture factorial); Python hash randomization disabled (PYTHONHASHSEED $=0$); all API calls use idempotent request parameters.
-
-\textbf{Memory architectures.} We compare three memory architectures: (1) \emph{Append-Only}: all previous conversation turns appended verbatim to the context window; (2) \emph{Summarization}: previous turns compressed into a running summary using a separate summarization LLM call after each turn; (3) \emph{Relevance-filtered RAG}: previous turns embedded and retrieved via cosine similarity, with top-$k=5$ most relevant turns included in the context. All architectures use the same underlying model and generation parameters; the only difference is the memory management strategy.
-
-\textbf{Contamination protocol.} Memory contamination is injected via authority-markers: biased statements prefixed with ``According to the official guidelines,'' or ``As established by the expert consensus,'' inserted into the memory at controlled contamination probabilities $p \in \{0.2, 0.5, 0.8\}$. The authority-markers are designed to trigger conformity bias in LLMs. Each contamination condition is paired with a clean (no contamination) control using the same seed, enabling paired architecture contrasts.
-
-\textbf{Tasks and datasets.} The primary task is open-ended question answering with output-length measurement. Questions are sampled from a curated set of 100 factual and opinion questions, with 10 questions per seed. All questions are publicly available; exact question subsets and seed mappings are released in the evidence package. Dataset versions: GSM8K (v1.0, 2021 release) for cross-task validation, MMLU (v1.0, 2020 release) for multi-disciplinary QA, HumanEval (v1.0, 2021 release) for code generation.
-
-\textbf{Metrics.} The primary metric is $\Gamma_{\text{temporal}}$, the Wasserstein-1 distance (earth mover's distance) between the biased and clean normalized output-length distributions. Output length is measured as the number of tokens in the model response. Normalization uses z-score normalization within each architecture-condition pair. Secondary metrics include mean output length, output-length standard deviation, and refusal rate. All metrics computed at the per-seed level and aggregated across seeds with bootstrap confidence intervals ($B=5000$ resamples).
-
-\textbf{Wasserstein-1 calculation.} The Wasserstein-1 distance between two distributions $P$ and $Q$ is computed as $W_1(P,Q) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Pi(P,Q)} \mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\gamma}[|x-y|]$, where $\Pi(P,Q)$ is the set of all joint distributions with marginals $P$ and $Q$. For empirical distributions, we use the sorted-values method: $W_1 = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} |x_{(i)} - y_{(i)}|$, where $x_{(i)}$ and $y_{(i)}$ are the sorted output lengths from the biased and clean conditions. Implementation uses the SciPy \texttt{wasserstein\_distance} function (SciPy v1.10+).
-
-\textbf{Statistical procedures.} All confidence intervals are bootstrap intervals ($B=5000$ resamples). Pairwise contrasts use paired Welch's $t$-test (since each contamination condition is paired with a clean control using the same seed) with Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons (correction factor $k=9$ for the nine predeclared pairwise contrasts). Effect sizes reported as Cohen's $d_z$ (paired Cohen's d). All tests two-sided unless otherwise noted.
-
-\textbf{Exclusion criteria.} Runs with API errors, empty responses, or malformed outputs ($<2\%$ of total) are excluded and re-run with fresh seeds. All exclusions logged in the evidence manifest with SHA-256 checksums for all result files.
